  <div class="side-note"><strong>Three priorities from your class files</strong><p><b>KU3</b> — text-type conventions<br><b>Ap2</b> — precise, integrated evidence<br><b>An2</b> — perceptive analysis of effects</p></div>
 </aside>
 <main class="main"><div class="topline"><span class="crumb" id="breadcrumb">Dashboard / Overview</span><span class="pill">OFFLINE STUDY RESOURCE</span></div><div class="content">
 <section id="home" class="view active">
  <div class="hero"><div class="eyebrow">Your exam preparation starts here</div><h1>Read closely.<br>Think critically.<br><em>Write with purpose.</em></h1><p>An interactive revision space based on your three class resources, with original poetry, prose, and drama extracts to practise Critical Reading.</p><div class="actions"><button class="btn darkprimary go" data-go="practice">Start practising →</button><button class="btn ghost go" data-go="learn">Revise techniques</button></div></div>
  <div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">The assessment focus</div><h2>What makes an A-level response?</h2></div></div>
  <div class="cards"><article class="card"><span class="tag">KU3</span><div class="mini-stat" style="margin-top:16px">01</div><h3>Know the form</h3><p class="muted small">Demonstrate detailed understanding of a <b>range</b> of stylistic features and conventions specific to poetry, prose and drama.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="tag">Ap2</span><div class="mini-stat" style="margin-top:16px">02</div><h3>Prove each claim</h3><p class="muted small">Use precise details and <b>short quotations woven fluently</b> into your own sentences. Every quotation needs a purpose.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="tag">An2</span><div class="mini-stat" style="margin-top:16px">03</div><h3>Explain the impact</h3><p class="muted small">Analyse <b>how and why</b> techniques influence interpretation, position readers and strengthen an idea.</p></article></div>
  <div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">The core method</div><h2>TEA: Technique → Evidence → Analysis</h2><p>Identify the writer's deliberate choice, prove it, then explore what it achieves.</p></div></div>
  <div class="steps"><div class="step"><span class="n">T</span><strong>Technique</strong><span class="small muted">“The poet personifies the clock…”</span></div><div class="step"><span class="n">E</span><strong>Evidence</strong><span class="small muted">“…through the phrase ‘chewed through another minute’…”</span></div><div class="step"><span class="n">A</span><strong>Analysis</strong><span class="small muted">“…suggesting that time is consuming the speaker's remaining opportunity.”</span></div><div class="step"><span class="n">↗</span><strong>Extend</strong><span class="small muted">“This invites readers to recognise how departure can feel irreversible.”</span></div></div>
  <div class="section-head"><div><h2>The difference between identifying and analysing</h2></div></div>
  <div class="explain-grid"><div class="example analysis-bad"><span class="tag amber">Weak</span><p class="big-quote">“The poet uses personification to make it more interesting.”</p><p class="small muted">Names a device, but gives no meaningful evidence, idea or reader effect.</p></div><div class="example analysis-good"><span class="tag">Stronger</span><p>“By personifying the clock as it <b>‘chewed through another minute’</b>, the poet transforms ordinary passing time into a threatening force, emphasising the speaker's anxiety about leaving.”</p><p class="small muted">Links the specific technique to the words, the idea, and a possible emotional effect.</p></div></div>
  <div class="section-head"><div><h2>Choose how to revise</h2></div></div>
  <div class="cards"><div class="card"><span class="tag gray">01 · Learn</span><h3 style="margin-top:10px">Technique dictionary</h3><p class="small muted">Definitions, examples, filters and flip cards for essential metalanguage.</p><button class="btn soft go" data-go="learn">Open the library →</button></div><div class="card"><span class="tag gray">02 · Apply</span><h3 style="margin-top:10px">Three unseen texts</h3><p class="small muted">One poem, one prose passage and one playscript. Nine questions with guided model answers.</p><button class="btn soft go" data-go="practice">Open practice →</button></div><div class="card"><span class="tag gray">03 · Test</span><h3 style="margin-top:10px">Technique quiz</h3><p class="small muted">Multiple-choice questions with instant scoring and explanations.</p><button class="btn soft go" data-go="quiz">Take the quiz →</button></div></div>
 </section>
 <section id="learn" class="view">
  <div class="eyebrow">Revision notes and flashcards</div><h1>Master the techniques.</h1><p class="lede">In Critical Reading, <b>metalanguage</b> means the precise vocabulary used to discuss how a text works. Focus on the devices most relevant to the form of the text.</p>
  <div class="section-head"><div><h2>Search the technique dictionary</h2><p>Choose a text type or search for a technique. Examples below are original practice examples, not quotations from the class slides.</p></div></div>
  <div class="searchbar">⌕ <input id="termSearch" type="search" placeholder="Search e.g. enjambment, dialogue, irony…" aria-label="Search techniques"></div>
  <div class="filters" id="termFilters"><button class="chip active" data-filter="All">All features</button><button class="chip" data-filter="Poetry">Poetry</button><button class="chip" data-filter="Prose">Prose</button><button class="chip" data-filter="Drama">Drama</button><button class="chip" data-filter="Language">Shared language</button><button class="chip" data-filter="Persuasion">Persuasive / visual</button></div>
  <div id="termCount" class="small muted" aria-live="polite"></div><div id="termGrid" class="term-grid" style="margin-top:13px"></div>
  <div class="section-head"><div><h2>Flashcard challenge</h2><p>Try to explain the technique before flipping to reveal its definition and example.</p></div></div>
  <div class="flashcard" id="flashcard" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="Flip study flashcard"><div class="minor" id="flashLabel">What does this mean?</div><div class="front" id="flashFront"></div><div class="back" id="flashBack"></div><div class="minor" style="margin-top:20px">Tap / click / press Enter to flip</div></div>
  <div class="flash-controls"><button class="btn tiny" id="flashPrev">← Previous</button><div class="count" id="flashNumber"></div><button class="btn tiny" id="flashNext">Next →</button><button class="btn tiny soft" id="flashShuffle">Shuffle</button></div>
  <div class="section-head"><div><h2>Text-type quick reference</h2></div></div>
  <div class="cards"><div class="card"><span class="tag">Poetry</span><h3 style="margin-top:12px">Form + sound + imagery</h3><p class="small">Rhyme, rhythm, enjambment, stanza breaks, free verse, caesura, alliteration, sound patterns, figurative imagery.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="tag">Prose</span><h3 style="margin-top:12px">Voice + sequence</h3><p class="small">Narrative point of view, characterisation, temporal shifts, sentence length, dialogue, setting, juxtaposition and symbolism.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="tag">Drama</span><h3 style="margin-top:12px">Stage + performance</h3><p class="small">Stage directions, props, set design, lighting, dialogue, physicality, proxemics, entrances/exits and pauses.</p></div></div>
  <div class="callout amber" style="margin-top:23px"><b>Teacher's key warning:</b> If the question asks about <em>features of the text type</em>, discussing only generic techniques such as symbolism and characterisation is not enough. For a poem, discuss something distinctly poetic; for a playscript, analyse the choices a playwright makes for performance.</div>
 </section>
