const DATA={
 poem:{title:'Platform Four',type:'Poetry · Unseen extract',html:`<div class="poem">At the platform’s edge<br>the clock chewed through another minute.<br>Behind the glass, the city held its breath.<br><br>Mother waved once.<br>The train did not.<br>Its windows carried strangers<br>who knew exactly where to sit.<br><br>I held a paper ticket<br>and a whole house of unsaid things.<br>The announcement broke into pieces<br>against the high, iron roof.<br><br>When the doors closed,<br>the platform shrank to a photograph.<br>I watched its colours fade<br>until the tracks ran on<br>beyond what I could see.</div>`,questions:[
 {q:'What central idea about leaving does the poet explore?',length:'2–3 sentences',hint:'Consider the contrast between the speaker’s uncertainty and the indifferent train. Focus on the idea, not a list of techniques.',model:'The poem represents leaving as both an unavoidable movement forward and an emotionally difficult separation from familiarity. Although the train continues on its predetermined route, the speaker feels unprepared, burdened by feelings left unexpressed.'},
 {q:'How does the poet use language and poetic form to communicate the speaker’s anxiety?',length:'2 developed TEA paragraphs',hint:'Analyse “the clock chewed through another minute,” the short sentence “The train did not,” the poem’s free-verse form, or the enjambment near the ending. Link each feature to an idea.',model:'The poet personifies the clock through the verb “chewed”, presenting time as a force that consumes the speaker’s remaining moments before departure. This unsettling image transforms an ordinary station clock into a symbol of inevitability and positions readers to share the speaker’s anxiety. The poet also uses a sudden short, end-stopped line, “The train did not”, after “Mother waved once.” This contrast between human emotion and the train’s impersonal movement emphasises the speaker’s powerlessness, while the abrupt pause captures the finality of the separation.'},
 {q:'How does the poem’s ending influence the reader’s interpretation of the departure?',length:'1 developed paragraph',hint:'Think about the shrinking platform, photographic image and the last two lines running together. Does the ending suggest only sadness, or also an unknown future?',model:'At the ending, the platform “shrank to a photograph”, a metaphor that transforms a lived place into a distant memory. This suggests that departure creates emotional distance even before the journey is complete. The final enjambment, as “the tracks ran on / beyond what I could see”, carries the reader forward across the line break and leaves the destination unknown. Consequently, the ending balances the grief of separation with the possibility of an unfamiliar future.'}
 ]},
 prose:{title:'The Last Notice',type:'Prose · Unseen narrative extract',html:`<p>On Monday morning, the council notice appeared on the library door. It was printed on a single white sheet, but it seemed to fill every window.</p><p><em>Closure: Friday, 5 pm.</em></p><p>My grandfather read it twice. Then he straightened the corner, which had curled away from the glass, as though the problem were simply that somebody had hung the notice badly.</p><p>“They have probably made a mistake,” he said.</p><p>Inside, the library smelled of paper and the sharp polish Mrs Bell used on the old wooden desks. The computers along the back wall hummed. A child was learning to write her name in thick blue letters, pressing so hard that the pencil tore the page. Nothing looked as though it was ending.</p><p>Grandfather went straight to the history shelf. He had repaired it in 1986, after a storm had brought rain through the roof. He ran one finger along the timber, pausing at a notch where the wood had split. I had always thought of the shelf as furniture. He touched it like a person he had known for years.</p><p>At the counter, Mrs Bell was stacking returned books. She did not say good morning. Her usual bright cardigan lay folded on the chair behind her.</p><p>“Is there anything we can do?” I asked.</p><p>Grandfather shook his head before she could answer. “Decisions like this are made somewhere else.”</p><p>Mrs Bell looked up. “They held a public meeting last Thursday. Eight people came.”</p><p>He took his hand away from the shelf.</p><p>For a moment I heard only the computers, still humming as if nothing had changed. Then the little girl at the table held up her paper and called, “Look. That’s me.”</p><p>Grandfather glanced at the name, then at the notice beyond the door. He asked Mrs Bell for a pen.</p>`,questions:[
 {q:'How does the narrative characterise the grandfather and develop his response to the closure?',length:'2 developed paragraphs',hint:'Look at his careful handling of the notice and the shelf, his dismissive dialogue, and the change in his actions after the public meeting is mentioned.',model:'The writer initially characterises the grandfather through small, defensive actions. His attempt to straighten the notice, “as though the problem” were only its placement, reveals his reluctance to accept the decision. The simile that he touches the old shelf “like a person he had known for years” conveys his deep personal attachment to the library. However, his dismissive claim that decisions are made “somewhere else” is challenged by Mrs Bell’s revelation that only eight people attended a public meeting. The closing action, when he “asked Mrs Bell for a pen”, suggests a shift from resignation towards possible involvement. His changing physical behaviour makes this development visible without stating his feelings directly.'},
 {q:'How does the writer use setting and sensory detail to explore the value of the library?',length:'1–2 developed paragraphs',hint:'Consider the smell of paper and polish; the hum of computers; a child practising her name; the history shelf. Explain why the setting matters beyond physical description.',model:'The writer uses sensory imagery to present the library as a living community space rather than a mere building. The smell of “paper and the sharp polish” gives the setting a familiar physical texture, while the background hum of computers suggests ordinary activity continuing despite the closure notice. The image of a child learning to write her name demonstrates the library’s role in growth and education. By juxtaposing these everyday details with the stark bureaucratic notice, the writer encourages readers to question a decision that reduces the library’s social value to an administrative outcome.'},
 {q:'How does the final paragraph influence the reader’s understanding of the text’s message?',length:'1 developed paragraph',hint:'Focus on narrative sequencing, a child announcing “That’s me”, and the grandfather asking for a pen. The ending is suggestive rather than completely resolved.',model:'The ending connects the child’s proud declaration, “That’s me”, with the grandfather’s decision to request a pen. The child symbolises the community’s future, prompting him to look beyond private nostalgia towards the library’s continuing importance to others. The writer withholds an explicit explanation of what he will write, creating an open ending that invites readers to infer his growing willingness to act. This shifts the text’s emphasis from helplessness towards the possibility of civic responsibility.'}
 ]},
 drama:{title:'Before the Curtain',type:'Drama · Unseen playscript extract',html:`<p class="stagedir">A community hall, late evening. Rows of empty chairs face a faded red curtain. A single work light illuminates a handwritten poster: “FINAL PERFORMANCE — SATURDAY.” ELI, sixteen, stands by the exit holding a rolled-up petition. MAYA, the hall caretaker, is stacking chairs.</p><div class="speaker">ELI</div><p>There are two hundred names on this. Two hundred and four, actually.</p><div class="speaker">MAYA</div><p class="stagedir">[Without looking up.]</p><p>Then the paper is heavier than it looks.</p><div class="speaker">ELI</div><p>They are selling the building. I thought you might want to sign.</p><div class="speaker">MAYA</div><p>I've spent twenty-three years keeping these lights on. I know what they're doing.</p><p class="stagedir">[She places another chair on the stack. The legs scrape harshly against the floor.]</p><div class="speaker">ELI</div><p>Then why are you helping them clear it out?</p><p class="stagedir">[MAYA stops. A long pause. She looks towards the curtain.]</p><div class="speaker">MAYA</div><p>Because someone has to make sure no one gets hurt when they leave.</p><div class="speaker">ELI</div><p>And after that?</p><div class="speaker">MAYA</div><p>After that, the doors get locked.</p><p class="stagedir">[ELI sets the petition on the front chair. He walks towards the exit, then stops.]</p><div class="speaker">ELI</div><p>My brother performed here. He forgot every line. Everyone clapped anyway.</p><p class="stagedir">[MAYA reaches for the next chair, but her hand remains suspended above its back.]</p><div class="speaker">MAYA</div><p>He was the one with the cardboard crown.</p><div class="speaker">ELI</div><p>Yes.</p><p class="stagedir">[MAYA crosses the space between them and unrolls the petition beneath the work light.]</p><div class="speaker">MAYA</div><p>Have you got a pen?</p><p class="stagedir">[The work light remains on. Blackout.]</p>`,questions:[
 {q:'How do dialogue and stage directions construct Maya’s character?',length:'2 developed paragraphs',hint:'Analyse the clash between her words and movements. Focus on her avoiding eye contact, the pause, the suspended hand and final movement.',model:'The playwright initially presents Maya as guarded through the stage direction “[Without looking up]”, suggesting that she is reluctant to confront Eli’s challenge. Her practical statement that “someone has to make sure no one gets hurt” characterises her as responsible but also allows her to justify inaction. Later, the direction that her hand “remains suspended” externalises her internal conflict. Her final decision to move towards the petition transforms hesitation into visible action, positioning the audience to regard her as someone capable of change.'},
 {q:'How do set design, props and lighting communicate the central conflict?',length:'1–2 developed paragraphs',hint:'Use the empty chairs, faded curtain, final-performance poster, petition, single work light and blackout. Think about what can be seen on stage.',model:'The sparse set design, with “rows of empty chairs” facing a “faded red curtain”, establishes a once-active cultural space now threatened with disappearance. The poster announcing the final performance makes the hall’s closure concrete, while Eli’s petition becomes a physical symbol of collective resistance. The “single work light” initially exposes the fragile setting, but at the end it illuminates Maya as she unrolls the petition. This shifting significance allows the playwright to associate the same limited light with a small but meaningful possibility of hope.'},
 {q:'How does the ending position an audience to view individual responsibility?',length:'1 developed paragraph',hint:'Analyse Maya’s change, “Have you got a pen?”, and the work light remaining on. Consider why the ending is not a complete resolution.',model:'Maya’s final question, “Have you got a pen?”, replaces her earlier fatalism with a willingness to participate in collective action. This brief dialogue is effective because the playwright lets the audience recognise a change in character through a concrete decision rather than an explanatory speech. The stage direction that the work light “remains on” before blackout symbolically preserves hope, while the unresolved fate of the hall reminds viewers that responsibility begins with action but does not guarantee success.'}
 ]}
};
const TERMS=[
 ['Metaphor','Language','Describes one thing as another without “like” or “as”.','“Her silence was a locked door.” — suggests an emotional barrier.'],
 ['Simile','Language','Explicit comparison using “like” or “as”.','“The corridor stretched like a tunnel.” — emphasises distance or unease.'],
 ['Personification','Language','Gives a non-human object, idea or force human attributes.','“The clock chewed through another minute.” — time seems aggressive.'],
 ['Symbolism','Language','An object, action, image or place represents a larger idea.','An unopened letter could symbolise uncertainty or avoidance.'],
 ['Juxtaposition','Language','Places contrasting elements close together to sharpen a difference.','A cheering crowd beside a grieving character highlights isolation.'],
 ['Imagery','Language','Sensory detail that evokes sight, sound, touch, smell or taste.','“The sharp polish on the wooden desks” makes setting immediate.'],
 ['Connotation','Language','The emotional or cultural associations a word carries beyond its definition.','“Dragged” suggests effort, reluctance or heaviness.'],
 ['Tone','Language','The author’s, narrator’s or speaker’s attitude to their subject.','Words such as “bleak” and “hollow” may establish a mournful tone.'],
 ['Repetition','Language','Repeating words, phrases or structures to add emphasis or rhythm.','“Still here. Still waiting. Still afraid.” increases insistence.'],
 ['Irony','Language','A contrast between expectation and reality, or between literal wording and intended meaning.','“Excellent timing,” she said as the train departed.'],
 ['Allusion','Language','Reference to another text, event, belief or widely known figure.','A reference to Icarus can imply ambition and reckless risk.'],
 ['Rhetorical question','Persuasion','A question designed to encourage reflection or agreement rather than obtain an answer.','“What kind of community closes its only library?” challenges the reader.'],
 ['Inclusive language','Persuasion','Pronouns that invite a shared identity or responsibility.','“We must decide what matters.” connects speaker and audience.'],
 ['Emotive language','Persuasion','Word choice intended to evoke emotion.','“Our abandoned neighbours” encourages sympathy and concern.'],
 ['Foregrounding','Persuasion','Making an image, idea or object visually or verbally prominent.','A brightly coloured poster is placed centrally in an advertisement.'],
 ['Anaphora','Poetry','Repetition at the beginning of successive lines or clauses.','“I remember the door. / I remember the light.” emphasises memory.'],
 ['Enjambment','Poetry','Continuation of a poetic sentence across a line break without a grammatical stop.','“The tracks ran on / beyond what I could see.” pushes the reader forward.'],
 ['Caesura','Poetry','A pause within a poetic line, commonly signalled by punctuation.','“I wanted to go — but stayed.” interrupts the flow to suggest doubt.'],
 ['End-stopped line','Poetry','A poetic line that ends with a grammatical pause or completed thought.','“The train did not.” creates a firm, abrupt ending.'],
 ['Free verse','Poetry','Poetry without a fixed metrical or rhyme scheme.','Uneven line lengths can reflect uncertainty or natural speech.'],
 ['Stanza break','Poetry','A space separating groups of poetic lines.','A new stanza may mark a change in time, mood or focus.'],
 ['Alliteration','Poetry','Repetition of consonant sounds at the beginning of nearby words.','“Slow shadows settled” can create a soft, drawn-out rhythm.'],
 ['Assonance','Poetry','Repetition of vowel sounds in nearby words.','“Low road home” links sounds and slows the line.'],
 ['Onomatopoeia','Poetry','A word that imitates a sound.','“Clatter” recreates the noise of an approaching train.'],
 ['Rhyme / rhyme scheme','Poetry','Similar ending sounds; a rhyme scheme is the pattern across lines.','An ABAB rhyme pattern can create cohesion or expectation.'],
 ['Narrative perspective','Prose','The position from which events are narrated, such as first person or third person.','“I watched the notice” restricts events to a narrator’s perspective.'],
 ['Characterisation','Prose','How a writer constructs a character through actions, dialogue, description and interaction.','Straightening a notice may reveal denial without directly naming it.'],
 ['Foreshadowing','Prose','A detail that hints at a later development.','An early mention of darkening clouds may signal later disruption.'],
 ['Flashback','Prose','A shift in narrative to an earlier time.','A remembered childhood scene may explain present attachment.'],
 ['Sentence structure','Prose','The length, arrangement and syntax of sentences and their effects.','“He stopped.” interrupts a long descriptive paragraph with finality.'],
 ['Stage directions','Drama','Instructions about movement, gesture, delivery or physical action on stage.','“[Without looking up.]” reveals avoidance without spoken explanation.'],
 ['Proxemics','Drama','How the spatial distance and positioning of performers convey relationships.','Moving across the stage towards another person may signal trust.'],
 ['Props','Drama','Physical objects handled or used by performers that can carry meaning.','The petition symbolises a chance for communal action.'],
 ['Lighting','Drama','Use of light, darkness and lighting changes to direct attention and suggest mood.','A single work light highlights vulnerability or fragile hope.'],
 ['Dialogue','Drama','Spoken exchange that reveals conflict, relationships and attitudes.','“Have you got a pen?” reveals changing commitment.'],
 ['Set design','Drama','The staged physical environment, including furniture and backdrop.','Empty chairs imply absence and a threatened community space.'],
 ['Dramatic pause','Drama','A deliberately indicated silence used to create tension or reveal hesitation.','“[A long pause.]” allows an audience to sense internal conflict.']
];
